**Definition 1.3 (joint continuation contract).** At a cut retain the actual
joint archive and reference $R$, including any quantum parts, and the actual
classical fields $M$: source and preparation identity, current height, clock
and alignment, calibration, controller and pending-programme state,
permissions, all acquired events, selectors, meters and update inputs. These
fields belong to one actual joint source. Their marginals are not independently
chosen, and none is replaced by a task summary.

The quantum state of the specimen may be correlated with $R$ and $M$.
For instance, arbitrary normal trace-class joint states and classical
state-valued measures are allowed. Every formula below acts on the specimen
and tensors with the identity on the retained registers. In particular, it
applies to entanglement with an inaccessible reference and to an actual
history-correlated archive; neither correlation is manufactured by the formula.

The future operation menu comprises every permitted prescribed original
control with matching current height, using the canonical complete stopped
instrument, and every finite chronological composition of these controls.
Policies may choose a next supplied control using actually acquired records,
the retained controller and matched external inputs. They use the same
measurable selection rule in the two representations. All original signs,
finite durations and both regularity alternatives remain in this menu.
Local operations on retained registers and their complete recorded updates
are also retained. A permission rejection is an unexecuted event; an executed
failure has the absorbing successor specified below. There is no reset,
post-failure original trial, extra specimen or reversed execution permission.

The conditional services in this contract are an actual prescribed waveform
generator, calibrated implementation of the specified Kraus operations,
blank ancillary systems, coherent encoding and decoding, the required clocks,
and the declared event-recording service. Exact failure-time readout is an
ideal real-valued service. None is acquired from an unknown specimen by
naming a control. A controller may retain information it actually possesses;
it is never supplied unknown source parameters merely to make a comparison.

Terminal quantum reads are performed after decoding. An additional
intermediate intervention with a quantum successor outside the invariant
support used below requires a new closure analysis. Such an intervention is
not part of the supplied canonical original-control instrument. The theorem
does not identify a formal inverse with permission to execute it.

